Chapter: CritterCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter CritterGold

 Grade Safari chapter Critter, which draws the animals' baked stills.

 A CHECK OF ITS OWN FOR A BUNDLE-SIZE REASON, not a logical one. The two generated
 tables are 216 KB and 534 KB of Codex literals; folded into RenderCheck on top of
 everything that check already cites, codexzig aborts outright -- not a diagnostic,
 a core dump. Apart they transpile comfortably. That is PORTING_NOTES C7 shaping
 the port's STRUCTURE rather than just a tolerance, for the second time.

 Both draw functions ARE pub, so both seams are real oracles: the probe calls the
 game's own function and reads the words it put on paint's wire.

 STRUCTURE EXACTLY, COORDINATES WITH THE PIXEL GATE. A still is baked art scaled by
 one number, so a wrong point count is a wrong polygon and every colour is a
 literal carried through unchanged; neither takes any tolerance.

 ALL EIGHT SPECIES ARE GRADED NOW, and the bull is the reason this paragraph used
 to say the opposite. It is the only Fluent COLOR animal and 40 of its 43 polygons
 carry a 2-stop gradient, which paint.zig writes as wire tags 5 and 6. While
 Safari chapter Paint modelled tags 0, 1 and 3 the generated table flattened each
 gradient to its first stop, so the port emitted tag 0 where the game emits 5 or
 6 -- two different wire shapes, and comparing them would have proved nothing.
 Paint has the tags, the baker carries the stops and the geometry, and Critter has
 the arm that emits them.

 THE BULL IS DRAWN TWICE, FACING BOTH WAYS, and the mirrored case is the one worth
 having. A gradient's axis is a VECTOR and its centre is a POINT: mirroring flips
 the axis without translating it, and mapping an axis as a point would still
 produce a polygon of exactly the right shape with its shading anchored somewhere
 else. `cr-geom` is the stream where that shows, and it is why the gradient's own
 numbers do not ride with the polygon's.

 ONLY THE BULL'S COORDINATES ARE THINNED. Adding it took the coordinate stream
 from 5,916 Reals to 15,152, close enough to C7's ceiling to be asking for the
 abort; every third point of the bull brings it to 9,044. The other seven species
 stay exact, and they exercise the same mapping code, so what the thinning costs
 is density on art whose density is already proven elsewhere.

 THE CAT'S COORDINATES ARE SAMPLED EVERY THIRD POINT and that is C7 again: seven
 stills are 13,453 points, 26,906 Reals, and one Real literal past about 16,868
 exhausts the transpiler's bump heap. Point counts stay exact, so a still that
 gained or lost a polygon fails hard regardless; only the density is thinned.
 Mountains hit the same ceiling and took the same answer.

 THE CAT'S HOP IS WHAT ITS CASES ARE FOR. It is applied in the UNIT FRAME -- added
 to each polygon's own y before scaling -- so it is measured in cat-heights and
 shrinks with distance like the rest of the still. Reading it as a metres offset to
 the anchor would look nearly right up close and drift wrong as the cat recedes,
 which one near sample would wave through. Two of the seven cases are airborne, at
 different depths.

 We say:

Section: The Cases

  CritterCase = record {
    cp : Integer,
    h : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    face : Boolean
  }

  critter-cases : List CritterCase
  critter-cases = [CritterCase { cp = 129414, h = 0.9, fwd = 14.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128024, h = 2.8, fwd = 40.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 129426, h = 4.5, fwd = 90.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 129427, h = 1.6, fwd = 25.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 129423, h = 2.2, fwd = 60.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128004, h = 1.4, fwd = 18.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 128022, h = 1.1, fwd = 9.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128002, h = 1.7, fwd = 22.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128002, h = 1.7, fwd = 22.0, face = True }]

  bull-cp : Integer
  bull-cp = 128002

  case-wire : Integer -> List DrawCmd
  case-wire (i) =
    let c = list-at critter-cases i
    in critter-draw 2.5 c.fwd c.h c.cp c.face focal camera-w

  critter-wire : Integer -> List DrawCmd
  critter-wire (i) =
    if i >= list-length critter-cases then []
    else (case-wire i) & critter-wire (i + 1)

Section: Flattening

  s-tags : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-tags (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & s-tags cs (i + 1)

  s-cols : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-cols (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color] & s-cols cs (i + 1)

  s-counts : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-counts (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [list-length (list-at cs i).pts / 2] & s-counts cs (i + 1)

  s-cols2 : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-cols2 (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color2] & s-cols2 cs (i + 1)

 Only tags 5 and 6 define `geom`, so concatenating every command's is the gradient
 stream and nothing else -- a solid fill contributes an empty list.

  s-geoms : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  s-geoms (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).geom & s-geoms cs (i + 1)

 Codex has no `%`, so a modulo is spelled the way Tower spells it: n - (n/d)*d.
 x and y are kept together so the pairing survives the thinning.

  keep-nth : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  keep-nth (k) (n) = k - (k / n) * n == 0

  every-nth : List Real, Integer, Integer -> List Real
  every-nth (ps) (i) (n) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length ps then []
    else if keep-nth (i / 2) n then [list-at ps i, list-at ps (i + 1)] & every-nth ps (i + 2) n
    else every-nth ps (i + 2) n

  thin-cmds : List DrawCmd, Integer, Integer -> List Real
  thin-cmds (cs) (n) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (every-nth (list-at cs i).pts 0 n) & thin-cmds cs n (i + 1)

 THE STEP IS PER CASE, not per check: the bull's stream is sampled and the other
 seven are whole.

  case-step : Integer -> Integer
  case-step (i) = if (list-at critter-cases i).cp == bull-cp then 3 else 1

  s-coords : Integer -> List Real
  s-coords (i) =
    if i >= list-length critter-cases then []
    else (thin-cmds (case-wire i) (case-step i) 0) & s-coords (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-tag  " (s-tags (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-tag)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-col  " (s-cols (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-col)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-col2 " (s-cols2 (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-col2)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-cnt  " (s-counts (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-cnt)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "cr-geom " (s-geoms (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-geom 0.001 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "cr-xy   " (s-coords 0) g-cr-xy 0.001 0.000001)
  end
